"The surah begins with an oath by the dawn (fajr). Fajr is the cracking open of the night's darkness and the emergence of the first whiteness of morning. With this oath, attention is drawn to the transition from darkness to light and to the Lordship of the Creator. The prefixed 'lam' may denote genus (jins) or specific reference ('ahd); if it denotes genus, it signifies every dawn, and if it denotes specific reference, it signifies a blessed dawn, such as the morning of the Feast of Sacrifice (Eid al-Adha).\n\nThe second oath is by the 'ten nights.' The strongest view is that these are the first ten nights of Dhu al-Hijjah. The proof for this was narrated from Jabir via a hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). There is also the possibility of them being the last ten nights of Ramadan or the tenth of Muharram. The purpose is to emphasize the value of arduous hours of worship that end in celebration and joy.\n\nThe third oath is by 'the even and the odd.' Shaf' means even, and watr means odd. There are many interpretations regarding this expression. According to Mujahid, the even is everything created, and the odd is Allah. In another hadith, the even and the odd are interpreted as the even and odd rak'ahs of prayer. According to the narration from Ibn Abbas, however, the even is the day of the Feast of Sacrifice, and the odd is the day of Arafah. This is the most consistent both with the order of the verses and with the oath by the ten nights.\n\nThe fourth oath is by 'the night as it departs.' It points to the time of dawn (sahar), when the darkness of the night is about to come to an end. It has also been said to be the night of Muzdalifah. All these oaths constitute a momentous oath for those of understanding, because they point to Allah's oneness and Lordship.\n\nThe answer to the oath is the verse, 'Indeed, your Lord is ever watchful.' Allah will punish the disbelievers. To see this, it is sufficient to look at the stories of 'Ad, Thamud, and Pharaoh.\n\nHow did Allah destroy the people of 'Ad? They were the dwellers of the city of Iram. The epithet 'Zat al-'Imad' means possessors of pillars. The people of 'Ad were a tall, powerful people who constructed immense buildings. The city of Iram is also recounted as a legendary paradisiacal garden built by Shaddad, but this is a fabrication.\n\nAs for the people of Thamud, they used to carve out houses from the rocks in the valley. They too were subjected to torment. Pharaoh, on the other hand, is referred to as 'the owner of stakes'; this is an allusion to the pyramids and to his might. All of this shows that those who transgress in this world cannot escape Allah's watchful surveillance.\n\nHuman beings misunderstand Allah's test. Whenever Allah honors a person and bestows favors upon him, he says, 'My Lord has honored me.' And whenever He restricts his provision and brings him distress, he says, 'My Lord has humiliated me.' In truth, both are tests; worldly blessing is not an absolute honor, nor is hardship an absolute humiliation.\n\nO you people! You do not honor the orphan, nor do you encourage feeding the poor. You consume inheritance voraciously, and you love wealth with an exceeding love. All of this stems from not reflecting upon the Hereafter.\n\nYet when the earth is violently shaken and leveled, when your Lord comes with the angels rank upon rank, and when Hell is brought forth, man will understand. On that Day, he will say, 'If only I had sent forth good deeds for my life!' but it will be of no avail. None can punish as Allah punishes, and none can bind as He binds.\n\nAs for the reassured soul (al-nafs al-mutma'innah) that devotes itself to Allah and finds serenity in Him, it will be addressed: 'O reassured soul! Return to your Lord, well-pleased and pleasing to Him, and enter among My servants, and enter My Paradise.'\n\nIn these verses, it is stated that the reassured soul (al-nafs al-mutma'innah) is addressed on the Day of Resurrection. According to the flow of the discourse, while on that Day the disbelieving person laments, saying, 'If only I had sent forward something for my life,' Allah the Exalted will call out to those who believed and attained peace of heart: 'O reassured soul!' This rhetorical style is one of the aspects of the Quran's inimitability (i'jaz) and instantly prepares the listener for that moment.\n\nIn verses 28–30, this soul is told: 'Return to your Lord, both well-pleased and pleasing [to Him].' The concepts of 'radiyyah' and 'mardiyyah' mean that the person is pleased with Allah and Allah is pleased with him. Then follow the commands: 'enter among My servants' and 'enter My Paradise.' Commentators have explained this entering as joining the company of sincere, righteous servants and entering Paradise alongside them. According to another interpretation, it refers to pure souls reflecting upon one another, thereby augmenting their radiance and ultimately attaining Paradise.\n\nThe concept of 'nafs' mentioned in the verse has been examined in detail. The nafs is the essence and reality of man by which he says 'I'; it has two dimensions: the perceiver (sha'ir) and the perceived (mash'ur). These two dimensions can separate from each other in states such as sleep, heedlessness, or death. A person's full harmony with his soul is possible only within the reality of Allah, which transcends him. Indeed, the verse 'Allah intervenes between a person and his heart' points to this. As for the spirit (ruh), it is by the command of the Lord and is taken by Allah in death and sleep. For this reason, al-nafs al-mutma'innah means the reassured essence or spirit.\n\nRegarding the definition of al-nafs al-mutma'innah, al-Razi presents three aspects: First, it is the taste of believing in the Truth with absolute certainty (yaqin) without any doubt. Second, it is attaining a security that cannot be shaken by fear and grief. Third, it is the heart finding rest solely through the remembrance of Allah. This serenity is attained either by reaching the Supreme Cause in intellectual reasoning and settling upon it, or by realizing that the servant's infinite need can only be fulfilled by Allah's infinite perfection. Whoever chooses Allah not for anything else but purely for Himself, that is the reassured soul.\n\nRegarding when this address will be made, there are three views: at the moment of death, at the time of resurrection after death, or when the reckoning is completed. According to the context of the discourse, the most apparent view is that it occurs on the Day of Reckoning. However, it is reported in traditions that an angel will recite this verse to Abu Bakr at the moment of death. A narration from Zayd ibn Aslam states that good tidings are given to the reassured soul in all three places: at death, at resurrection, and on the Day of Gathering. These views can be reconciled; indeed, the day of death is also considered a part of the Resurrection.\n\nSufis have classified the stages of the soul: the commanding soul (al-nafs al-ammarah), the self-reproaching soul (al-nafs al-lawwamah), the reassured soul (al-nafs al-mutma'innah), the pleased soul (al-nafs al-radiyyah), the pleasing soul (al-nafs al-mardiyyah), the inspired soul (al-nafs al-mulhamah), and the purified soul (al-nafs al-zakiyyah). In this surah, first the condition of the commanding soul and the self-reproaching soul is described, followed by the salvation of the reassured soul. Some commentators have stated that the command 'return' applies at every moment in this world as well. According to this, in every matter, the believer should turn back to Allah being pleased with Him and His decree, thereby joining the company of righteous servants in this world and becoming worthy of entering Paradise in the Hereafter."